# EIP-1271 isValidSignature Spoof

## Overview
EIP-1271 lets smart contract accounts validate their own signatures. Unlike EOA signatures, validity is determined by a call into signer-controlled code, so the verifier must treat a successful call as untrusted until the exact magic value is checked.

There are two commonly encountered function variants with separate magic values:

```solidity
// Current EIP-1271 variant. Valid only if it returns bytes4(0x1626ba7e).
function isValidSignature(bytes32 hash, bytes memory signature) external view returns (bytes4 magicValue);

// Legacy bytes-hash variant. Valid only if it returns bytes4(0x20c13b0b).
function isValidSignature(bytes memory data, bytes memory signature) external view returns (bytes4 magicValue);
```

Do not conflate EIP-1271 with EIP-712. EIP-712 defines how structured data is hashed and displayed for signing; EIP-1271 defines how a contract signer reports whether a signature is valid for a supplied hash or data blob.

## Threat Model
The verifier is the protocol accepting a signature-gated action. It chooses the expected signer, computes the hash or data, calls `isValidSignature`, and must enforce the correct magic-value result.

The signer can be an arbitrary contract account. A benign signer may be a Safe or another smart wallet with owner/module policy. A malicious signer can implement `isValidSignature` to approve every hash and signature, approve stale signatures, or return the wrong variant's magic value to exploit verifier confusion.

Counterfactual and pre-deployment wallets add more complexity. EIP-6492 wraps signatures for accounts that may not be deployed yet; verifiers that support it must unwrap and deploy/check the account path safely before applying the same strict EIP-1271 magic-value validation.

## Detection
Search for `isValidSignature(bytes32`, `isValidSignature(bytes`, `0x1626ba7e`, and `0x20c13b0b`. Review each verifier-side call and each wallet-side implementation separately.

Flag verifier code when any of the following holds:

- It treats a non-reverting `isValidSignature` call as valid without comparing the return value.
- It calls `isValidSignature` without `try/catch` or low-level failure handling, allowing malicious or incompatible wallets to revert and deny service.
- It compares against the wrong magic value for the function variant being called.
- It checks only that the returned `bytes4` is non-zero.
- It uses `abi.decode(returndata, (bool))`, `success == true`, or `returndata.length > 0` as the validity condition.
- It supports EIP-6492 counterfactual signatures but does not re-run strict EIP-1271 validation after resolving the counterfactual signer.

Flag signer/wallet code when `isValidSignature` returns a magic value without binding validation to the requested hash or data, signer policy, nonce, expiry, and domain expected by the application.

## Exploitation
A malicious wallet can return the EIP-1271 magic value for every input. If the verifier lets users choose the signer contract or assumes contract signatures are self-authenticating, arbitrary calls can pass authorization.

```solidity
interface IERC1271 {
    function isValidSignature(bytes32 hash, bytes calldata signature) external view returns (bytes4);
}

contract MaliciousWallet is IERC1271 {
    bytes4 internal constant MAGIC_VALUE = 0x1626ba7e;

    function isValidSignature(bytes32, bytes calldata) external pure returns (bytes4) {
        return MAGIC_VALUE; // Approves every hash and every signature.
    }
}
```

The bug usually appears in verifier code that checks only call success, or that makes the call but ignores the returned selector.

```solidity
interface IERC1271 {
    function isValidSignature(bytes32 hash, bytes calldata signature) external view returns (bytes4);
}

contract BadVerifier {
    function verify(address signer, bytes32 hash, bytes calldata signature) external view returns (bool) {
        // Missing try/catch: incompatible or malicious wallets can revert this verifier path.
        signer.staticcall(
            abi.encodeWithSelector(IERC1271.isValidSignature.selector, hash, signature)
        );

        // Wrong: call success or lack of revert is not signature validity.
        return true;
    }
}
```

If a protocol uses the result to mark hashes as consumed, mint assets, execute orders, or authorize withdrawals, an attacker can register or pass a malicious contract signer and have every attempted signature accepted. Replay protection inside the verifier is not enough when the signer contract says every replayed hash is valid and the verifier does not require the exact magic value for the intended variant.

## Mitigation
Compare the return value exactly against the magic value for the function variant being called. Use `try/catch` or a well-reviewed helper such as OpenZeppelin `SignatureChecker` so reverts, short return data, and wrong magic values are handled as invalid signatures, not as success.

```solidity
interface IERC1271 {
    function isValidSignature(bytes32 hash, bytes calldata signature) external view returns (bytes4);
}

contract GoodVerifier {
    bytes4 internal constant ERC1271_MAGIC_VALUE = 0x1626ba7e;

    function verify(address signer, bytes32 hash, bytes calldata signature) external view returns (bool) {
        if (signer.code.length == 0) {
            return false; // EOA path should use ECDSA recovery separately if supported.
        }

        try IERC1271(signer).isValidSignature(hash, signature) returns (bytes4 value) {
            return value == ERC1271_MAGIC_VALUE;
        } catch {
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```

For legacy `isValidSignature(bytes,bytes)`, compare against `bytes4(0x20c13b0b)` instead. Do not accept either magic value interchangeably.

Additional hardening:

- Bind the signed message to a nonce, expiry, `block.chainid`, and verifying contract where the protocol controls the signed payload.
- Restrict which contract signers are trusted when authorization depends on a known wallet policy.
- Treat EIP-6492 support as a wrapper around signer resolution, not as a replacement for strict EIP-1271 return-value checks.
- Prefer OpenZeppelin `SignatureChecker.isValidSignatureNow` for mixed EOA and contract-signature support, while remembering that contract signatures are revocable and can change over time.

## References
- EIP-1271: Standard Signature Validation Method for Contracts — https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-1271
- EIP-6492: Signature Validation for Predeploy Contracts — https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-6492
- OpenZeppelin SignatureChecker — https://docs.openzeppelin.com/contracts/4.x/api/utils#SignatureChecker
- Safe Smart Account Signatures — https://docs.safe.global/safe-smart-account/contract-and-implementation-details/signatures

